    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> of 1815 had one of the most liberal constitutions of 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but the Russian emperors as its kings disregarded the limits on their power.</li><li>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a> of 1830–1831 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a> of 1863 its autonomy was dismantled and the land was ruled directly as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, officially called Vistula Land.</li><li>Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> became the most industrialised region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, with the textile centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodz">Łódź</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> the empire's third-largest city.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In January 1831 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> deposed Nicholas I as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, answering his repeated curtailing of its constitutional rights; he sent Russian troops into the country.</p></aside>
    </section>

