    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Between 1772 and 1795 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> in three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partitions-of-poland">Partitions of Poland</a>. In 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> created the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> as a French client state from land taken from Prussia, and enlarged it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s expense after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wagram">Battle of Wagram</a> in 1809. After his defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812, Prussia insisted that the duchy be eliminated, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> wished to annex it; Austria and the United Kingdom, through Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castlereagh">Castlereagh</a>, strongly opposed him.</p><p>In 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> took the Duchy of Warsaw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, gave part of it to Prussia as the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a> and made the rest, with Warsaw, a nominally independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> ruled by the Russian tsar. Historians call it Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after the congress. It owed much to Adam Jerzy Czartoryski, who sought to revive the Polish state in alliance with Russia. With 128,500 km2 and about 3.3 million people at first, it was one of the smallest Polish states ever, smaller than the Duchy of Warsaw. Its population was mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, ethnic Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, with a small Russian minority, and mainly Roman Catholic.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Agriculture made up 70% of the national income, and most farms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/folwark">folwarks</a>, were large noble estates worked by landless peasants; exports of wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">rye</a> stabilised the finances, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> was an important buyer. From 1822 Prince Francis Xavier Drucki-Lubecki opened the Polish market to the world, built the Augustów Canal between the Narew and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> to bypass Danzig and Prussian tariffs, and founded the Bank Polski. The first Polish steam mill was built in Warsaw-Solec in 1828 and the first textile machine installed in 1829.</p><p>The government drew foreign specialists, mostly Germans, relieved of taxes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodz">Łódź</a> and towns such as Ozorków and Zduńska <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wola">Wola</a> grew into one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s largest textile centres, multicultural cities where Germans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were the majority. Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> became the most industrialised region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and Europe's largest supplier of zinc. After the customs border was abolished in 1851, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, with its railways and bridges, gained priority in the Russian market and became the empire's third-largest city. The population reached 6.1 million by 1870 and 9,402,253 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 1897.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under the constitution of 1815 the kingdom had an independent army of its own. Discontent at the rule of Nicholas I broke into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/november-uprising">November Uprising</a>: when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> deposed him as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in January 1831, he sent Russian troops in, and the insurrection was defeated after an 11-month military campaign. The Polish Army was then disbanded, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> faced confiscation of property, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a>, forced military service and the closing of their universities. The governor Field Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-paskevich">Ivan Paskevich</a> directly controlled the Administrative Council.</p><p>In 1863 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a> broke out, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> signed the Alvensleben Convention on 8 February; the rising lasted two years before it was crushed. Later the governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, who commanded the Warsaw Military District, controlled all military forces in the region and could impose death sentences without trial. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the Russian army first won victories against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, but after the Gorlice–Tarnów offensive its Great Retreat of 1915 looted and abandoned the kingdom, and it deported hundreds of thousands of inhabitants suspected of collaborating with the enemy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> of 1815 made the emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> king, with a viceroy (namiestnik), a Council of State, an Administrative Council of five ministers and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> that could vote laws. The kingdom had its own currency, budget, penal code and a customs border with Russia, and was one of the few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchies</a> of its day. In practice the kings held absolute power and the title of Autocrat. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> gave the viceroy, Grand Duke Konstantin Pavlovich, almost dictatorial powers, abolished press freedom in 1819, ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freemasonry">Freemasonry</a> abolished in 1821, and from 1825 the Sejm met in secret, while Nikolay Novosiltsev's secret police pursued Polish societies.</p><p>Nicholas I, crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> on 24 May 1829, pursued Official Nationality: Orthodoxy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">Autocracy</a> and Nationality. After 1831 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regulamentul-organic">Organic Statute</a> of 1832 repealed the constitution; voivodeships became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">governorates</a> in 1837, the ruble became legal tender in 1841 and the Russian penal code replaced the Polish one. After 1863 Russian became co-official, and in the 1880s Polish was banned from official use and schools. The last namiestnik, Friedrich Wilhelm Rembert von Berg, died in 1874, and the governor-general of Warsaw took his role.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> held most of the ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, and its history shows the wider fate of the partitioned Polish lands, alongside the Austrian Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodomeria">Lodomeria</a> and the Prussian Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poznan">Posen</a>. Its liberal constitution, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> and the nobility's tradition of Golden Liberty made the absolute rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> wanted hard to establish, and its two great uprisings, in 1830 and 1863, were answered with ever closer integration into Russia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> bore on religion as well as language: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> was persecuted, most monasteries were closed and nationalised, papal bulls could not be read without the government's consent, and in 1875 the Uniate Eparchy of Chełm-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belz">Belz</a> was united with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>. The land reform of 1864, which gave the peasants ownership, was meant to win the peasantry's loyalty and break the economic power of the rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>. The kingdom's industry made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodz">Łódź</a> major cities, and its territory corresponds roughly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a>, Łódź, Masovian, Podlaskie and Holy Cross Voivodeships of Poland and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom lost its autonomy in stages. After 1831 it kept the złoty and some privileges of the Administrative Council for a time, but its separate status was gradually eroded; the customs border went in 1851. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/january-uprising">January Uprising</a> removed what separate status remained, and the land was incorporated directly into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>. From 1867 official Russian usage called it Vistula Land (Privislinskiy kray), to remove Polish national identity from the map, although it was still formally the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and the tsars kept the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. In 1912 the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Chełm</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> was split off and attached to the Southwestern Krai to speed its russification.</p><p>Russian rule effectively ended with the Great Retreat of 1915, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a> occupied the region. The Germans set up the Government General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, and in 1916 it was succeeded by a short-lived Kingdom of Poland, a client state of the Central Powers with a Regency Council instead of a king. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> of March 1918, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, by then in civil war, ceded all the Polish lands it had held to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Congress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> set up by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 under the Russian tsar, with a liberal constitution that its kings ignored. After the uprisings of 1830 and 1863 it was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as Vistula Land, even as it became the empire's most industrialised region, until Russian rule ended in 1915.</em></p>

